#!/usr/bin/env bash
#
# mutation-probe — plant a violation, run a test filter, report, revert.
#
# Mutation testing is how this project measures whether a guard is sensitive to
# the thing it claims to watch: plant the violation, expect red, remove it. Done
# by hand it has a failure mode that reads exactly like success, and it happened
# here on 2026-09-03: a planted `.onReceive(timer) { … }` referenced a symbol the
# file did not have, so the target never compiled, ZERO tests ran, and a harness
# that grepped for failing test names printed nothing — which is
# indistinguishable from "the guard is green". The plant was recorded as "not
# caught" when it had never been tried.
#
# So this script reports nine RESULT lines and never conflates them (counted in the
# code below, 2026-09-03 — an earlier version of this header said three while
# the code had five; corrected again 2026-09-04 — the five-label list below folded
# the two RED endings into one "RED / GREEN" line, so it enumerated five labels for
# seven lines. Split to match, one label per `echo "RESULT:` line. Nine since
# 2026-10-15, when the control run added two; re-counted with
# `/usr/bin/grep -c '^ *echo "RESULT:' scripts/mutation-probe`, which prints 9 —
# anchored on the line start so that this comment cannot match its own pattern):
#
#   CONTROL NOT         the UNPLANTED tree did not build, ran zero tests, or exited
#     ESTABLISHED         non-zero with no failing test named; stops BEFORE planting
#   PLANT FAILED       the --apply command exited non-zero; nothing was measured
#   PLANT CHANGED       the --apply command matched nothing; a silent no-op
#     NOTHING
#   BUILD FAILED        the plant does not compile; no test ran; the probe is void
#   NO TESTS RAN         the build succeeded but the filter matched nothing
#   RUN INCOMPLETE        the planted run ran FEWER tests than the unplanted one:
#                           it did not finish, and is neither red nor green
#   RED (caught)          tests ran, and a failure the unplanted run did not have
#                           is named
#   RED (uncaught)         tests ran and `swift test` exited non-zero on a tree with
#                           no pre-existing failure, but no verdict line names
#                           which test
#   GREEN                   tests ran, nothing new failed, and none of them noticed
#                            the plant
#
# `NO TESTS RAN` has a second cause, found 2026-09-04 (the
# ceilings-under-other-spellings plan, Task 2): a narrow --filter can
# produce a red whose run SUMMARY line never reaches stdout at all — the
# same interleaved-output loss docs/BACKLOG.md calls "CI logs lose
# lines" — so the old RAN regex read 0 about a run that had, in fact, run
# and failed. `swift test --event-stream-output-path` writes one JSON
# event per line to a file that stdout race cannot touch; RAN and the
# caught-test list are read from THAT file first (`testEnded` events
# counted, `issueRecorded` events' `testID`s named), and the stdout parse
# only runs as a fallback when the event file comes back empty.
#
# RAN counts test FUNCTIONS, not `@Test(arguments:)` CASES and not
# suites — both measured 2026-09-04 (fix round 1) against
# `CLIMatrixITests.swift`'s `everyConnectionKindHasARigFixture(kind:)`, a
# 3-case parameterised test: the event file carried 3
# `testCaseStarted`/`testCaseEnded` pairs (a different event kind, not
# counted here) but only ONE `testEnded` for the function, aggregating
# all 3 cases into one verdict. The same run's event file also carried a
# SECOND `testEnded`, for the suite itself — undetected through fix round
# 1, which counted it into RAN: a 7-test suite read RAN 8 in every RESULT
# line that round reported. A suite's `testID` carries no parentheses; a
# test's always does (even a zero-argument test's signature ends `()`),
# so RAN now counts only `testEnded` events whose `testID` contains one.
#
# EVERY VERDICT IS READ AGAINST A CONTROL (added 2026-10-15). Before it plants
# anything the script runs the same filter on the unplanted tree and records how
# many tests ran (CONTROL_RAN) and which already fail (CONTROL_FAILED). Three
# defects in the verdict logic, each measured on f0269692 before the change:
#
#   1. A KNOWN issue counted as a catcher. The caught-test list was every
#      `issueRecorded` event's `testID`, with no look at `issue.isKnown`, so a
#      test using `withKnownIssue` would be named as "the plant was caught by"
#      in every run, including those where nothing caught the plant. Measured:
#      `git show f0269692:scripts/mutation-probe | /usr/bin/grep -c isKnown`
#      prints 0. (No `withKnownIssue` in the tree then: latent, not live.)
#   2. No failure baseline. Any failure already present in the filtered scope was
#      reported as the plant's catcher. Live in this tree: the test "Varying only
#      isRegex leaves the pixels unchanged" in `ViewTestabilitySpike.swift`
#      fails with nothing planted, and
#      `git show f0269692:scripts/mutation-probe > scripts/.mutation-probe-old`
#      then, after `chmod +x` on it, `scripts/.mutation-probe-old --filter
#      ViewTestabilitySpike --apply "echo '// x' >> Sources/MacSCPAppKit/AppMain.swift"`
#      printed `RESULT: RED — 7 test(s) ran, and the plant was caught by:` and
#      named that test, for a plant that is a comment.
#   3. RAN never compared to anything. `NO TESTS RAN` fired only on 0, so a plant
#      that stops the runner after some tests had ended left a partial event
#      file with RAN positive. Measured 2026-10-15 with an untracked test that
#      ends the process only when a marker file exists, filter
#      `ComparisonDiffView|<that suite>`, old script: a `fatalError` printed
#      `RED — 56 test(s) ran and 'swift test' exited 1, but no verdict line`
#      (the status branch caught it, but as a RED that reads like a catch); an
#      `exit(0)` printed `GREEN — 56 test(s) ran, 'swift test' exited 0`. The
#      control ran 57 both times. The second is a crash read as an all-clear:
#      the build-failure bug, one step along.
#
# So the caught-test list is now the planted run's failures MINUS the control's
# (pre-existing failures are printed apart, labelled, never as a catcher); a
# KNOWN issue is not a failure in either run; and RAN falling below CONTROL_RAN
# is its own ending. A control that cannot be established stops the script
# before the plant: a verdict against no baseline is a verdict about nothing.
# The price is a probe that takes about twice as long, on purpose.
#
# Failures are compared by testID with the trailing declaration location
# (`/File.swift:LINE:COL`) removed. Measured 2026-10-15: the event file's
# testID for a test carries it (`.../ViewTestabilitySpike.swift:194:6`), so a
# plant that edits a TEST file above a failing test moves its line, and the
# control's failure would no longer match itself — the pre-existing failure
# would come back as a catcher. The cost is deliberate: the printed names no
# longer say where the test is declared. Suite and function name still identify
# a test within one module, so nothing is ambiguous, but a reader who wants the
# line has to look the test up.
#
# What a control cannot do: a test that ALREADY fails cannot report that the
# plant also breaks it, so a plant caught only by a pre-existing failure reads
# GREEN. The GREEN text says so whenever the control had a failure.
#
# A signal is NOT one of these endings: on INT or TERM the script reverts and
# exits non-zero WITHOUT a verdict (130 / 143). It used to trap with
# a handler that RETURNED, so bash resumed at the next statement and the run
# continued against the reverted tree — printing "GREEN, and none of them
# noticed the plant" about a tree that no longer carried one. Observed
# 2026-09-03 by sending SIGINT during `swift test`: the old script printed
# exactly that and exited 0; this one exits 130 with no verdict.
#
# Verifying that by hand needs job control. A shell that starts this script
# with `&` and no `set -m` sets SIGINT to SIG_IGN for it, and bash cannot trap
# a signal that was ignored on entry — so the handler never runs and the run
# looks unfixed. An interactive Ctrl-C, which is the case that matters, has job
# control on.
#
# It also refuses to start on a dirty tree, and checks that the plant changed
# something — a `sed` that matched nothing is the same silent no-op one level up.
#
# Usage:
#   scripts/mutation-probe --filter <regex> --apply <shell command> [--label <text>]
#
# Example:
#   scripts/mutation-probe --label "teardown stops nothing" \
#     --filter 'DiagnosticsLifecycleTests' \
#     --apply "perl -0pi -e 's/^ *stopDiagnostics\(of: tab\)\n//m' \
#              Sources/MacSCPAppKit/ContentView+Lifecycle.swift"
#
# The tree is restored with `git checkout --` on every exit path, including a
# Ctrl-C: a probe that leaves its violation behind is worse than no probe.
#
# (--help prints lines 2-146 of this file: the header, and not the code under
# it. The range is a line number, so it is checked whenever this header grows.)

set -uo pipefail

# The event-stream read below (`jq -c`/`jq -r` against
# --event-stream-output-path) is silent on a missing binary under `set -o
# pipefail` alone — `jq: command not found` lands on stderr of a pipeline
# whose last stage still runs, so RAN reads empty, falls through the same
# path an empty event file takes, and the probe reports "NO TESTS RAN"
# about a run that in fact ran and was never inspected. Checked here,
# before anything is planted, so the failure names its actual cause.
command -v jq >/dev/null || { echo "mutation-probe: jq is required"; exit 2; }

FILTER=""
APPLY=""
LABEL=""

while [[ $# -gt 0 ]]; do
    case "$1" in
        --filter) FILTER="${2:-}"; shift 2 ;;
        --apply)  APPLY="${2:-}";  shift 2 ;;
        --label)  LABEL="${2:-}";  shift 2 ;;
        -h|--help) sed -n '2,146p' "$0"; exit 0 ;;
        *) echo "mutation-probe: unknown argument '$1'" >&2; exit 2 ;;
    esac
done

if [[ -z "$FILTER" || -z "$APPLY" ]]; then
    echo "mutation-probe: --filter and --apply are both required" >&2
    exit 2
fi

cd "$(dirname "$0")/.." || exit 2

# UNSTAGED changes only. `git checkout -- .` restores the working tree from the
# INDEX, so staged work is what the plant is reverted back TO — which is the
# normal case while a fix is in progress. What cannot be tolerated is an
# unstaged edit, because the revert would take it with the plant.
#
# Which is why the INDEX itself is saved below and put back before the
# revert. Measured 2026-09-27, during the guards-cluster task: an --apply
# that ran `git checkout HEAD -- <paths>` — a perfectly ordinary way to
# measure an old guard against a new plant — writes the INDEX as well as the
# working tree, so `git checkout -- .` then restored HEAD and the staged work
# this gate promises to preserve was gone. The script did not even report it
# as a failure: the post-plant check came back empty and blamed the pattern
# ("PLANT CHANGED NOTHING") for work it had destroyed. `git write-tree` /
# `git read-tree` makes the revert index-safe for ANY --apply, not only for
# that spelling. The blobs survive in the object store either way — the round
# lost to this was recovered with `git fsck --unreachable` — but a probe must
# not need that.
if [[ -n "$(git diff --name-only)" ]]; then
    echo "mutation-probe: the working tree has unstaged changes." >&2
    echo "  This script reverts with 'git checkout --', which would discard them." >&2
    echo "  Stage (git add) or stash them first; staged work is preserved." >&2
    git diff --name-only | sed 's/^/    /' >&2
    exit 2
fi

BASELINE="$(git rev-parse --short HEAD)"
# The index as it stands before anything is planted, so `restore` can put it
# back whatever --apply did to it (see the note above the dirty-tree gate).
INDEX_TREE="$(git write-tree)"
restore() {
    [[ -n "${INDEX_TREE:-}" ]] && git read-tree "$INDEX_TREE" 2>/dev/null
    git checkout -- . 2>/dev/null
    if [[ -n "$(git diff --name-only)" ]]; then
        echo "mutation-probe: WARNING — unstaged changes remain after the revert." >&2
        git diff --name-only | sed 's/^/    /' >&2
    fi
}

# EXIT restores and lets the exit status stand; a SIGNAL restores and EXITS,
# because a handler that returns hands control back to the statement after the
# interrupted one. Traps are cleared inside the handler so the EXIT trap does
# not run the revert a second time.
on_signal() {
    trap - EXIT INT TERM
    restore
    echo "" >&2
    echo "mutation-probe: interrupted by SIG$1 — the plant is reverted and NOTHING" >&2
    echo "  was measured. There is no verdict; run it again." >&2
    exit "$2"
}
trap restore EXIT
trap 'on_signal INT 130' INT
trap 'on_signal TERM 143' TERM

# Runs the filtered suite and leaves its figures in SUITE_RAN, SUITE_FAILED,
# SUITE_STATUS and SUITE_LOG (the caller removes the log). One function, so the
# control run and the planted run are read by the SAME code: a difference between
# their figures is then a difference between the trees, not between the readings.
run_suite() {
    SUITE_LOG="$(mktemp)"
    local events
    events="$(mktemp)"
    swift test --filter "$FILTER" --event-stream-output-path "$events" >"$SUITE_LOG" 2>&1
    SUITE_STATUS=$?

    # RAN and FAILED are read from the event stream first, not from stdout.
    # Measured 2026-09-04 (Task 2 of the ceilings-under-other-spellings plan,
    # `--filter theConnectDeadlineAlsoCoversNameResolution` against a planted
    # 90 s connect timeout): the filtered run's red was real — one issue
    # recorded, the test failed after 90.005 seconds — but the job's own
    # stdout log stopped mid-way through the test's doc-comment echo, with no
    # `✘ Test run with N tests` summary line for the old RAN regex to find.
    # RAN came back 0 and this script reported NO TESTS RAN about a run that
    # had, in fact, run and caught the plant — the exact "CI logs lose lines"
    # shape (docs/BACKLOG.md) reproduced locally and deterministically.
    # `--event-stream-output-path` writes one JSON object per event to a file
    # the stdout race cannot touch, the same flag and the same jq shape
    # `.github/workflows/ci.yml`'s "Test verdicts from the event stream" step
    # already reads for the identical reason: `issueRecorded` events name what
    # failed by `testID`.
    #
    # RAN counts test FUNCTIONS, not `@Test(arguments:)` CASES and not
    # suites — both measured 2026-09-04 (fix round 1), same probe run
    # (`--filter everyConnectionKindHasARigFixture`, `CLIMatrixITests.swift`,
    # `@Test(arguments: ConnectionKind.allCases)`, 3 cases): the event stream
    # carried 3 `testCaseStarted`/`testCaseEnded` pairs (no `messages`, so no
    # verdict text) but only ONE `testEnded` for the function itself, whose
    # message read "... with 3 test cases passed ..." — the per-case events
    # are a different `payload.kind` this script does not read, and a
    # parameterised function's N cases collapse into one `testEnded`. The
    # SAME run's event file also carried a SECOND `testEnded`, for the suite
    # itself, testID `macSCPCoreTests.CLIMatrixCoverageTests` with no
    # parentheses and a message starting "Suite "; counting it went
    # unnoticed through fix round 1 (every RESULT line that round reported
    # was one too high — a 7-test suite read RAN 8). A test's testID always
    # carries its function signature's parentheses; a suite's never does, so
    # the filter below excludes any testID without a `(`.
    SUITE_RAN=0
    SUITE_FAILED=""
    if [[ -s "$events" ]]; then
        SUITE_RAN="$(jq -c 'select(.kind == "event" and .payload.kind == "testEnded"
                                   and (.payload.testID | test("\\(")))' "$events" 2>/dev/null | wc -l | tr -d ' ')"
        SUITE_RAN="${SUITE_RAN:-0}"
        # A KNOWN issue (`withKnownIssue`) is an expected failure and not a
        # catch, so `isKnown` is filtered in the control run and the planted
        # run alike (header, defect 1). A WARNING is not a failure either, and
        # it DOES arrive as an `issueRecorded` event. Measured 2026-10-15 with
        # an untracked test whose body was
        # `Issue.record("just a warning", severity: .warning)`: the run exited
        # 0 and printed "passed ... with 1 warning", and the event carried
        # `"isFailure":false,"isKnown":false,"severity":"warning"` — so
        # `isKnown` alone did not exclude it, and the unfiltered extraction
        # listed that test as a PRE-EXISTING FAILURE of the control (and would
        # have named it as the plant's catcher had the warning appeared only
        # in the planted run). `isFailure != false` drops it; an event with no
        # `isFailure` field at all (an older toolchain) is still kept, because
        # unknown is not a reason to stop counting a failure. The `sub` removes
        # the declaration location from the testID so the two runs' names can
        # be compared (header, the paragraph on testID locations); a name
        # without one is left alone.
        SUITE_FAILED="$(jq -r 'select(.kind == "event" and .payload.kind == "issueRecorded"
                                      and .payload.issue.isKnown != true
                                      and .payload.issue.isFailure != false)
                               | (.payload.testID // empty)
                               | sub("/[^/]*\\.swift:[0-9]+:[0-9]+$"; "")' \
            "$events" 2>/dev/null | sort -u)"
    fi

    # Falls back to the stdout parse ONLY when the event file is empty — an
    # older toolchain that does not accept the flag, or a run that died before
    # writing anything to it. A nonempty event file is trusted over stdout
    # even when stdout also carries its own summary line, so the two derivations
    # never have a chance to disagree silently.
    if [[ ! -s "$events" ]]; then
        SUITE_RAN="$(grep -oE 'Test run with [0-9]+ test' "$SUITE_LOG" | grep -oE '[0-9]+' | tail -1)"
        SUITE_RAN="${SUITE_RAN:-0}"
        # Anchored on the marker plus a space, not on `[a-zA-Z]`: swift-testing
        # prints a DISPLAY-named test as `✘ Test "some name" recorded an
        # issue`, and the character after `Test ` is then a quote. This tree
        # carries 255 `@Test("…")` declarations (counted 2026-09-03,
        # `grep -rhoE '@Test\("[^"]*"' Tests/ | wc -l`), so the old class
        # excluded the majority spelling. The run SUMMARY (`✘ Test run with N
        # tests failed…`) has to come back out, or every red is attributed to
        # it instead of to a guard — which is the fallback path's own version
        # of the bug the event stream exists to route around.
        SUITE_FAILED="$(grep -E '^✘ Test ' "$SUITE_LOG" \
            | grep -vE '^✘ Test run with' \
            | grep -vE ' skipped(\.|:.*)?$' \
            | sed 's/ recorded.*//;s/ failed.*//' | sort -u)"
    fi
    rm -f "$events"
}

# The CONTROL: the same build and the same filter on the tree as it stands
# before anything is planted (staged work included, which is the baseline the
# plant is measured against). Its ending is a RESULT line of its own, because a
# control that could not be established is not a baseline, and a plant applied
# on top of one would produce a verdict about nothing.
control_void() {
    echo "RESULT: CONTROL NOT ESTABLISHED — $1"
    echo "  Nothing was planted. A verdict needs a baseline to be read against."
    shift
    for line in "$@"; do [[ -n "$line" ]] && printf '%s\n' "$line" | sed 's/^/  /'; done
    exit 1
}

echo "── control build (unplanted tree)"
CONTROL_BUILD_LOG="$(mktemp)"
if ! swift build --build-tests >"$CONTROL_BUILD_LOG" 2>&1; then
    errors="$(grep -E 'error:' "$CONTROL_BUILD_LOG" | sort -u | head -5)"
    rm -f "$CONTROL_BUILD_LOG"
    control_void "the unplanted tree does not build." "$errors"
fi
rm -f "$CONTROL_BUILD_LOG"

echo "── control test --filter $FILTER"
run_suite
CONTROL_RAN="$SUITE_RAN"
CONTROL_FAILED="$SUITE_FAILED"
CONTROL_STATUS="$SUITE_STATUS"
if [[ "$CONTROL_RAN" == "0" ]]; then
    errors="$(grep -E 'error:|Fatal|fatalError' "$SUITE_LOG" | sort -u | head -5)"
    rm -f "$SUITE_LOG"
    control_void "the filter matches no test on the unplanted tree, or the runner died." "$errors"
fi
# A non-zero status with no failing test named is a control that crashed or
# failed without a verdict line: its figures are not a baseline, and the planted
# run's non-zero status could not be told apart from it.
if [[ "$CONTROL_STATUS" -ne 0 && -z "$CONTROL_FAILED" ]]; then
    errors="$(grep -E '✘|error:|Fatal|fatalError' "$SUITE_LOG" | sort -u | head -5)"
    rm -f "$SUITE_LOG"
    control_void "the unplanted run exited $CONTROL_STATUS, but no failing test is named." "$errors"
fi
rm -f "$SUITE_LOG"

if [[ -z "$CONTROL_FAILED" ]]; then
    echo "── control: $CONTROL_RAN test(s) ran, 0 pre-existing failures"
else
    echo "── control: $CONTROL_RAN test(s) ran, $(printf '%s\n' "$CONTROL_FAILED" | wc -l | tr -d ' ') pre-existing failure(s):"
    echo "$CONTROL_FAILED" | sed 's/^/     /'
fi

# The no-op check below reads `git diff` as "the plant changed something", which
# only holds if nothing else has touched the tree since the dirty-tree gate. The
# control build now runs between the two, so it is checked here instead of
# assumed: a build that rewrote a tracked file (a resolved-packages file, say)
# would otherwise let a plant that matched nothing look like one that worked.
if [[ -n "$(git diff --name-only)" ]]; then
    echo "mutation-probe: the control run modified tracked files, so a plant could not" >&2
    echo "  be told apart from it. Nothing was planted." >&2
    git diff --name-only | sed 's/^/    /' >&2
    exit 2
fi

echo "── plant${LABEL:+: $LABEL}"
if ! eval "$APPLY"; then
    echo "RESULT: PLANT FAILED — the --apply command exited non-zero. Nothing was measured."
    exit 1
fi

if [[ -z "$(git diff --name-only)" ]]; then
    echo "RESULT: PLANT CHANGED NOTHING — the pattern did not match."
    echo "  A probe that plants nothing looks exactly like a guard that caught nothing."
    exit 1
fi
git --no-pager diff --stat | sed 's/^/  /'
echo "── (baseline: staged tree at $BASELINE)"

echo "── build"
BUILD_LOG="$(mktemp)"
if ! swift build --build-tests >"$BUILD_LOG" 2>&1; then
    echo "RESULT: BUILD FAILED — no test ran, so this probe measured NOTHING."
    echo "  Fix the plant so it compiles, then run again. A green report here would be a lie."
    grep -E 'error:' "$BUILD_LOG" | sed 's/^/  /' | sort -u | head -5
    rm -f "$BUILD_LOG"
    exit 1
fi
rm -f "$BUILD_LOG"

echo "── test --filter $FILTER"
run_suite
RAN="$SUITE_RAN"
FAILED="$SUITE_FAILED"
STATUS="$SUITE_STATUS"
TEST_LOG="$SUITE_LOG"

# What the PLANT added: the planted run's failures minus the control's. Both
# lists are `sort -u`, and `comm` needs both sorted the same way, hence the
# explicit LC_ALL=C on the three commands. `printf '%s'` rather than `echo`, so
# an empty list is an empty input and not one blank line.
CATCHERS="$(LC_ALL=C comm -23 <(printf '%s' "$FAILED" | LC_ALL=C sort -u) \
                              <(printf '%s' "$CONTROL_FAILED" | LC_ALL=C sort -u))"

# Printed under every ending that reaches a verdict, so a failure that was
# already there can never be read as the plant's catcher.
print_preexisting() {
    [[ -n "$CONTROL_FAILED" ]] || return 0
    echo "  Pre-existing failures (also failing on the UNPLANTED tree — NOT the plant's catchers):"
    echo "$CONTROL_FAILED" | sed 's/^/    /'
}

if [[ "$RAN" == "0" ]]; then
    echo "RESULT: NO TESTS RAN — the filter matched nothing, or the runner died."
    grep -E 'error:|Fatal|fatalError' "$TEST_LOG" | sed 's/^/  /' | sort -u | head -5
    rm -f "$TEST_LOG"
    exit 1
fi

# A planted run that ran FEWER tests than the control did not finish. Measured
# 2026-10-15 (the defect-3 case in the header): a process ended during one test,
# with the same filter on a tree where it was not ended, left an event file
# holding the tests that had finished before it died — RAN positive, nothing
# named — and the old script printed GREEN when the process exited 0 and a
# RED with no attribution when it trapped. Neither is the truth: the plant
# stopped the runner, which is neither a catch nor a survivor and is something
# the author of the probe has to look at. Any failure named before it stopped
# is listed, as information and not as a verdict.
if [[ "$RAN" -lt "$CONTROL_RAN" ]]; then
    echo "RESULT: RUN INCOMPLETE — the planted run did not finish."
    echo "  The test count fell from $CONTROL_RAN (unplanted) to $RAN (planted): the plant may"
    echo "  have trapped the runner. This is neither RED nor GREEN; read the log before"
    echo "  believing either."
    echo "  'swift test' exited $STATUS."
    if [[ -n "$CATCHERS" ]]; then
        echo "  Failures named before it stopped (not a verdict):"
        echo "$CATCHERS" | sed 's/^/    /'
    fi
    grep -E 'error:|Fatal|fatalError' "$TEST_LOG" | sed 's/^/  /' | sort -u | head -5
    print_preexisting
    rm -f "$TEST_LOG"
    exit 1
fi

# The exit status is read, not just the log. `swift test`'s own status is the
# most reliable signal available, and deciding red/green from the grep alone
# meant any failure that does not print a verdict line — a runner error after
# the summary, or the interleaved-output loss that cost CI run 33741778350 its
# only failing test's name — came out GREEN. That is the exact inference this
# script exists to refuse.
#
# With a pre-existing failure in the control the status is read differently,
# because `swift test` exits non-zero on THOSE alone: the status branch below
# applies only when the control was clean, and a control with failures that
# ends GREEN says so (a new failure without a verdict line would hide behind
# the old ones).
if [[ -n "$CATCHERS" ]]; then
    echo "RESULT: RED — $RAN test(s) ran, and the plant was caught by:"
    echo "$CATCHERS" | sed 's/^/  /'
    print_preexisting
elif [[ "$STATUS" -ne 0 && -z "$CONTROL_FAILED" ]]; then
    echo "RESULT: RED — $RAN test(s) ran and 'swift test' exited $STATUS, but no"
    echo "  verdict line names which test. The status is the verdict; the"
    echo "  attribution is missing. Read the full log before believing either:"
    echo "    $TEST_LOG   (kept; remove it when done)"
    grep -E '✘|error:' "$TEST_LOG" | sed 's/^/  /' | sort -u | head -10
    # This is the one ending whose whole message is "the attribution is
    # missing", and the log is the only place left to find it. It used to tell
    # the reader to read a log that the line below had already deleted
    # (measured 2026-10-15 by reading this branch: `$TEST_LOG` was named in
    # the text and `rm -f "$TEST_LOG"` ran at the end of the verdict block). So
    # this branch keeps the file and prints its path; every other ending
    # still removes it.
    KEEP_LOG=1
else
    echo "RESULT: GREEN — $RAN test(s) ran and NONE of them noticed the plant."
    echo "  Either the guard is insensitive to this violation, or the plant is not one."
    if [[ -n "$CONTROL_FAILED" ]]; then
        echo "  'swift test' exited $STATUS (control: $CONTROL_STATUS) because of the pre-existing failures"
        echo "  below. A plant caught ONLY by one of them cannot show here, and a new failure"
        echo "  that names no test would read the same as they do."
        print_preexisting
    else
        echo "  ('swift test' exited 0.)"
    fi
fi
[[ -n "${KEEP_LOG:-}" ]] || rm -f "$TEST_LOG"

echo "── revert"
exit 0
